# PostgreSQL / Data Migrations
- Apply PostgreSQL guidance only after verifying the repository actually uses PostgreSQL.
- Treat persistent data as non-replaceable; prefer additive, backward-compatible migrations and staged rollouts.
- Never drop/truncate production data, rewrite large tables, change column types in-place or remove constraints without explicit recovery planning.
- Consider transactions, locks, table size, backfill cost, index-build behavior and mixed-version compatibility.
- Preserve PK/FK/unique/nullability/referential-integrity semantics unless deliberately changing schema.
- Add indexes only for demonstrated access patterns and account for write/maintenance cost.
- Parameterize application SQL; never concatenate untrusted input.
- Make data migrations idempotent/checkpointable when practical and define partial-failure recovery.
- Verify migrations on a safe environment/test DB before calling them production-ready.
- Report whether forward migration, recovery/rollback, data preservation and relevant query behavior were actually verified.
